          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get heartburn most evenings after dinner. Is that worth mentioning to a doctor?",
          "answer": "Yes, it is worth mentioning to a doctor, especially because it happens most evenings. The pattern you describe, heartburn after meals, is the typical kind of reflux symptom. The \"GERD\" reference describes heartburn as a burning feeling behind the breastbone, often worse after meals and when lying down (*General clinical reference, Gastroesophageal reflux disease*).\n\n**Get emergency help now** if you have chest pain or pressure with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, pain spreading to the jaw, back or arm, or fainting. Heartburn and heart-related pain can feel alike, and a history of reflux does not rule out a heart cause. Antacid relief does not rule it out either (*Emergency medicine and cardiology guidance, Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*).\n\nFor the ongoing pattern, the sources describe some things that commonly contribute:\n- Large or late meals, and fatty meals, which slow stomach emptying (*Lifestyle: eating with reflux*).\n- Alcohol and carbonated drinks (*Lifestyle: eating with reflux*).\n- Excess body weight, smoking, and some medications that relax the lower oesophageal sphincter. Do not stop any medication on your own. Ask your doctor or pharmacist about it (*GERD condition reference*).\n\nA guideline summary says that for adults with typical symptoms and no warning signs, doctors often start with a trial of acid-reducing treatment before further tests. It does not give a specific treatment for you, so ask your doctor what suits your situation (*Gastroenterology guideline, GERD initial management*).\n\nWhen you see a doctor, it helps to note when the heartburn happens, what you ate, and what helps or worsens it. Mention any warning signs, such as trouble swallowing, unexplained weight loss, vomiting blood, or black stools. These sources do not list every warning sign, so I cannot confirm that your symptoms are safe to watch at home.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
